  "body": "_Edit: to those coming to this thread fresh, the OP may be confusing. The conclusion of this thread was that the spec was good as-is, but we needed to add tests because browsers were not consistent. The tests were added in https://github.com/w3c/web-platform-tests/pull/10717. Keep reading for the gory details._\r\n\r\nBecause it exposes a response URL for the script, the fragment should be censored, per [Fetch's definition of response URL](https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#response-url):\r\n\r\n> A response URL is a URL for which implementations need not store the fragment as it is never exposed. When serialized, the exclude fragment flag is set, meaning implementations can store the fragment nonetheless. \r\n\r\nHowever, it appears that specs need to carefully censor this themselves, e.g. like Fetch does in the [`response.url` getter](https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-response-url).\r\n\r\nHTML was not careful about this, and @bmeck has reported that this caused interop problems where Safari followed the HTML spec verbatim (and so included the fragment) whereas Chrome used their usual response-URL infrastructure (and thus didn't have a fragment around).\r\n\r\nAlmost certainly the right thing to do here is censor the fragment and add a WPT ensuring that it stays that way.\r\n\r\n/cc @annevk @whatwg/modules ",
